From a1ef15d0a347dab0138b996b4a9ec22a50d60cc6 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Velle Sinclair <brncomputerhelp@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 7 Aug 2026 17:01:59 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 2/2] Read the desktop's own theme, cursor and wallpaper

Three fields that could only ever describe a full desktop environment now
describe a bare Wayland compositor too.

Theme and Cursor both came out of ~/.config/gtk-3.0/settings.ini, which is
the wrong place to ask on a session whose compositor is not GTK's. Such a
compositor either does not write settings.ini at all (so the fields were
simply absent), or writes a generic light/dark stand-in into it AFTER the
user picked something -- so "Adwaita-dark" was reported on a desktop that
is actually on "dracula".

  - Theme consults a small table of compositor state files first, and says
    which one it read (`dracula [synui]`), falling back to GTK unchanged.
  - Cursor prefers XCURSOR_THEME/XCURSOR_SIZE. Those are what
    libwayland-cursor and every toolkit's Wayland backend actually load the
    pointer from, so where they are set they are the truth and the GTK keys
    are at best a copy of it.

Wallpaper is new, and off nothing: there is no standard for it, so it probes
the files the common setters write (hyprpaper, nitrogen, swww, synui) most
specific first and stops at the first hit. Plain file reads only -- a setter
that is not installed costs one failed fopen(), not a subprocess. Prints the
basename, because these are absolute paths several directories deep and the
info block shares its width with the logo.

Fields that find nothing still print nothing, so a desktop none of this
matches looks exactly as it did.
